syntax:
Compile-time computations are performed using templates and template specialization.  For example:

```c++
template <int N>
struct Factorial {
  static constexpr int value = N * Factorial<N - 1>::value;
};

template <>
struct Factorial<0> {
  static constexpr int value = 1;
};

int main() {
  constexpr int result = Factorial<5>::value; // Computed at compile time
  std::cout << result << std::endl; // Prints 120
  return 0;
}
```

Note:  `constexpr` emphasizes compile-time evaluation, though the compiler may choose to evaluate it at runtime in certain contexts.  Error handling within TMP is crucial as compile-time errors can be complex to diagnose.
